<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drigung Kagyu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Drigung <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> is a school of the Kagyu tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, founded by Jigten Sumgön, who established Drigung Thil monastery north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> in 1179. In the 13th century it was a major religious and political power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> until its defeat by the Sakya and their Mongol allies in 1290. It remains an important lineage, with monasteries in Tibet, Ladakh and elsewhere.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drigung-kagyu.jpg" alt="Drigung Kagyu" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drikung_Kagyu_Lineage_Tree.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
